.TH KINDUCE 1 "September 2026" "kinduce" "Tournament inducibility"
.SH NAME
kinduce \- decide whether a tournament is inducible by k voters
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kinduce
.RB { \-\-paley
.IR q " | " \fB\-\-bits\fR " " \fIfile\fR " " \fB\-\-n\fR " " \fIn " | " \fB\-\-batch\fR " " \fIfile\fR " " \fB\-\-n\fR " " \fIn }
.RB [ \-\-k
.IR k ]
.RB [ \-\-margin
.IR mode ]
.RB [ \-\-max\-margin
.IR M ]
.RB [ \-\-order
.IR mode ]
.RB [ \-\-inc ]
.RB [ \-\-base
.IR "v ..." ]
.RB [ \-\-bs\-from
.IR a ]
.RB [ \-\-bs\-to
.IR b ]
.RI [ break ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kinduce
decides whether a given tournament
.I T
on
.I n
vertices is the majority tournament of some
.I k
linear orders: that is, whether there are
.I k
rankings of the vertices such that for every arc
.IR "u \(-> v" ,
a majority of the rankings place
.I u
above
.IR v .
Such a set of rankings is a
.IR witness .
.PP
The search is a depth\-first placement of vertices into a partial profile. It
starts from a
.I base state
\(em the restriction of all
.I k
voters to a small base set, taken up to the relabelling of voters \(em and
inserts one vertex at a time. For each unplaced vertex it maintains the set of
slot tuples still consistent with every arc to the placed set, chooses the most
constrained vertex at every node, and refines those sets incrementally rather
than recomputing them.
.PP
The base states partition the search space, so a run may be sliced with
.B \-\-bs\-from
and
.B \-\-bs\-to
and the slices distributed across workers. A slice that is exhausted without a
witness refutes
.I T
on that slice; the union of all slices refutes
.I T
outright.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Instance
.TP
.BI \-\-paley " q"
Build the Paley tournament on
.I q
vertices, for
.I q
a prime with
.IR "q \(== 3 (mod 4)" .
Refuses composite or prime\-power
.IR q ,
where the
.RI Z/ q
construction does not yield a tournament; use
.B \-\-bits
with a proper field build in that case.
.TP
.BI \-\-bits " file " \-\-n " n"
Read
.I T
from a file holding the upper\-triangular adjacency bit string, row major:
.RI C( n ,2)
characters, a
.B 1
at position
.RI ( i , j )
meaning the arc runs
.IR "i \(-> j" .
.TP
.BI \-\-batch " file " \-\-n " n"
Read many tournaments, one bit string per line, and decide each in turn.
.TP
.BI \-\-k " k"
Number of voters. Must be odd. Default 5.
.SS Margin
.TP
.BI \-\-margin " majority|exact"
.B majority
requires every arc to be supported by at least
.RI ( k +1)/2
voters.
.B exact
requires exactly that many \(em every arc decided by the narrowest possible
margin.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-margin " M"
Require every arc's support
.I s
to satisfy
.RI | 2 s - k | " \(<= " M .
.I M
must have the same parity as
.I k
and lie in
.RI [1, k ].
.RB "For " k " = 5, " "\-\-max\-margin 1"
is equivalent to
.B "\-\-margin exact"
and
.B "\-\-max\-margin 5"
to
.BR "\-\-margin majority" .
.SS Search
.TP
.BI \-\-order " mrv"
Choose the next vertex per node by minimum remaining values. Recomputed at
every node, not fixed once per base state.
.TP
.B \-\-inc
Refine each unplaced vertex's domain from its parent's rather than
re\-enumerating. Worth roughly a factor of 110 and effectively required at
.IR n " \(>= 19."
.TP
.BI \-\-pool\-mb " M"
Size in megabytes of the arena holding incremental domains. Default 256.
Raise it if the run aborts with a pool\-exhausted error.
.TP
.BI \-\-mrv\-cap " N"
Stop counting a domain once
.I N
tuples have been seen, for the purpose of ranking candidates. Default 20000.
A cap only affects which vertex is chosen, never correctness.
.TP
.BI \-\-select " cheap|hybrid"
Rank candidates by the cheap per\-voter agreement score instead of, or blended
with, the full domain size.
.TP
.B \-\-fas
Enable the tier\-1 dead\-node test, which compares the dissent already committed
against
.RI k \(mu minFAS
of the unplaced part. Needs the subset DP table and therefore
.IR n " \(<= 26."
.TP
.BI \-\-fas2 " d"
Tier\-2 lookahead to depth
.IR d .
.TP
.BI \-\-tie\-fas " max|min"
Break ties in the vertex order by the FAS statistic. Implies
.BR \-\-fas .
.SS Decomposition
.TP
.BI \-\-base " v ..."
Name the base set explicitly. Without it the base is selected automatically by
scanning subsets, which at large
.I n
costs more than it saves and should be avoided in production runs.
.TP
.BI \-\-bs\-from " a" ", " \-\-bs\-to " b"
Restrict to base states with index in
.RI [ a , b ).
Slices are disjoint and their union is the whole space, so a set of slices
covering
.RI [0, "base_states" )
is a complete run.
.SS Symmetry breaking
See
.B SOUNDNESS
below: every option here carries an obligation the program cannot check.
.TP
.BI \-\-top0rr " r " r2
Require some voter to rank vertex 0 first and one of
.IR r ", " r2
second.
.TP
.BI \-\-toporb " r1 r2 ..."
Require some voter to rank one of
.IR r1 ", " r2 ", ..."
first. The representatives must meet every orbit of
.RI Aut( T )
on vertices.
.TP
.BI \-\-toppair " u1 v1 u2 v2"
Require some voter's (first, second) pair to be
.RI ( u1 , v1 )
or
.RI ( u2 , v2 ).
The two representatives must lie in
.I different
arc orbits \(em one an arc of
.IR T ,
the other a non\-arc. The program checks that much and refuses otherwise.
.SS Defect mode
.TP
.BI \-\-defect " file"
Look for a profile whose majority tournament is
.I T
with exactly one arc reversed, that arc confined to the set
.I R
read from
.IR file .
Used to decide arc\-criticality in a single run. Incompatible with
.BR \-\-fas / \-\-fas2 ,
because reversing an arc changes minFAS; the program refuses the combination.
.TP
.BI \-\-defect\-support " lo hi"
Constrain the reversed arc's support, measured in
.IR T "'s own direction, to [" lo , hi ].
.SS Output and limits
.TP
.B \-\-count
Count witnesses instead of halting at the first.
.TP
.B \-\-emit
With
.BR \-\-count ,
print every witness as it is found rather than only the total.
.TP
.BI \-\-time " s"
Abort after
.I s
seconds. An aborted run is
.I not
a refutation: check for a nonzero capped count before drawing any conclusion.
.SH OUTPUT
A run prints a configuration header, periodic
.B PROGRESS
lines, and one terminal line:
.PP
.RS
.B RESULT SAT
\(em a witness was found, and is printed above the result line together with a
.B VERIFY
line recomputing every arc's support independently of the search.
.br
.B RESULT UNSAT
\(em the slice was exhausted with no witness.
.RE
.PP
The
.B SLICE
line reports how many base states were cleared and how many were
.IR capped .
.B "A capped count above zero means the slice is incomplete"
and the run refutes nothing.
.SH SOUNDNESS
Three obligations rest on the caller, not on the program.
.TP
.B "Orbit representatives"
.B \-\-toporb
is sound only if the given representatives meet every orbit of
.RI Aut( T )
on vertices. The program cannot compute
.RI Aut( T )
and so cannot check this; a set that misses an orbit silently discards every
profile whose top vertices lie in it. Compute the orbits separately. Using the
orbits of a
.I subgroup
is safe, since they refine the true ones.
.TP
.B "Spending the group once"
The vertex breaks and the defect set
.I R
both consume
.RI Aut( T ).
Combining a full\-strength break with a restriction on where the defect may lie
spends the same group twice and is unsound; the program refuses an
.I R
that meets the base for this reason.
.TP
.B "Completeness of a distributed run"
A refutation requires every base state in
.RI [0, "base_states" )
to have been cleared, with none capped and none missing. Verify the index
cover, not merely the count.
.SH EXAMPLES
Refute 5\-inducibility of Paley(23), one slice:
.PP
.RS
.nf
kinduce \-\-paley 23 \-\-k 5 \-\-margin majority \-\-order mrv \-\-inc \\
        \-\-pool\-mb 512 \-\-base 0 1 2 5 11 \-\-top0rr 2 5 \\
        \-\-bs\-from 0 \-\-bs\-to 100
.fi
.RE
.PP
Decide Paley(43) minus a vertex at margin at most 3:
.PP
.RS
.nf
kinduce \-\-bits p43_minus1v.bits \-\-n 42 \-\-k 5 \-\-max\-margin 3 \\
        \-\-order mrv \-\-inc \-\-pool\-mb 512 \-\-base 0 1 2 3 10 \\
        \-\-toporb 0 1 \-\-bs\-from 0 \-\-bs\-to 8031
.fi
.RE
.PP
Enumerate every witness rather than stopping at the first:
.PP
.RS
.nf
kinduce \-\-bits p23_minus1v.bits \-\-n 22 \-\-k 5 \-\-margin majority \\
        \-\-order mrv \-\-inc \-\-count \-\-emit
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on a completed run, whatever the verdict; 1 on a usage or soundness refusal;
2 on an internal limit being exceeded, such as an exhausted domain pool.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR REPRODUCE.md ,
which maps each published result to a single command line, and
.BR versions/ ,
which retains the historical implementations each result was originally
produced with.
